# Gotcha configuration — all variables are read with prefix GOTCHA_.
# Copy this file to .env and adjust. See cmd/gotcha/config.go for the
# authoritative source of truth (defaults/validation) and README.md for
# a narrative walkthrough of the most important settings.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Core
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# HTTP listen address.
#
# Commented out on purpose: under docker compose the container listens on
# :8080 and the host port mapping (59080:8080) plus the container healthcheck
# both assume that address. Changing it in .env breaks both. Uncomment only
# when running the binary directly, without docker compose.
#GOTCHA_ADDR=:8080

# Public URL of this instance. Used to build project DSNs, alert/incident
# links, and invite links — must match how users actually reach the
# instance (scheme+host+port). Non-local (non-localhost) values force
# GOTCHA_SECRET_KEY to be a real secret (see Security below) and log a
# warning if not https://.
#
# Commented out on purpose: docker-compose.yml sets its own value
# (http://localhost:59080, matching the published port). Uncommenting this
# while running under compose OVERRIDES that value — with the port mismatched,
# every form submit is rejected as cross-origin. Uncomment only when running
# the binary directly, without docker compose.
#GOTCHA_BASE_URL=http://localhost:8080

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Database
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# PostgreSQL connection string (relational state: orgs, projects, users,
# issues, alert rules, incidents).
#
# Commented out on purpose: docker-compose.yml points the app at its own
# postgres container. Uncommenting this under compose would send the app to
# localhost INSIDE the container, where no database listens. Uncomment only
# when running the binary directly against your own PostgreSQL.
#GOTCHA_PG_DSN=postgres://gotcha:gotcha@localhost:5432/gotcha?sslmode=disable

# ClickHouse connection string (high-volume data: events, spans, metrics,
# profiles, uptime results).
#
# Commented out on purpose: same reason as GOTCHA_PG_DSN above — compose
# provides its own ClickHouse container and its own connection string.
#GOTCHA_CH_DSN=clickhouse://localhost:9000/gotcha

# Database passwords for the compose-provided containers (substituted into
# both the DB container init and the app DSN). NOTE: on an EXISTING install
# the container init variable has no effect — run ALTER USER in the database
# first, then change the value here (see /docs/configuration). URL-unsafe
# characters (@ / : # %) must not be used.
#GOTCHA_PG_PASSWORD=gotcha
#GOTCHA_CH_PASSWORD=gotcha

# Memory ceilings for the compose-provided database containers. Raise on a
# server with headroom; docker-compose.small.yml overrides them downwards.
#GOTCHA_PG_MEM_LIMIT=512m
#GOTCHA_CH_MEM_LIMIT=2g

# Memory ceiling of the app container itself.
#GOTCHA_MEM_LIMIT=1g

# MTU of the container network. Docker assumes 1500 without looking at the
# host's uplink, and a VPS behind a tunnel commonly has 1450 — the container
# then advertises an MSS the path can't carry, and large inbound packets are
# dropped silently. Small requests work while TLS handshakes time out, which
# hits email, webhooks, OAuth and uptime checks alike. Compare with
# `ip -o link show` on the host and match the external interface.
#GOTCHA_NET_MTU=1450

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Email (SMTP) — used for invite emails and the email alert channel.
# Email delivery is disabled until GOTCHA_SMTP_HOST is set.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

GOTCHA_SMTP_HOST=
GOTCHA_SMTP_PORT=587
GOTCHA_SMTP_USER=
GOTCHA_SMTP_PASSWORD=
GOTCHA_SMTP_FROM=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Telegram
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Base address of the Bot API. Empty means https://api.telegram.org.
# Set it when the instance cannot reach api.telegram.org — traffic filtering
# on the way out, a closed egress, or your own telegram-bot-api server next
# to the instance. Must be an absolute http(s) URL without query or fragment;
# the sender appends /bot{token}/sendMessage to it.
#GOTCHA_TELEGRAM_API_BASE=https://telegram.internal.example.com

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Retention (ClickHouse data, in days)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Events / transactions / Web Vitals retention (uptime check results share
# it too). 0 keeps data forever.
GOTCHA_RETENTION_DAYS=90

# Trace spans retention. 0 keeps data forever.
GOTCHA_SPAN_RETENTION_DAYS=30

# Metric points retention. 0 keeps data forever.
GOTCHA_METRIC_RETENTION_DAYS=30

# Profile samples retention. 0 keeps data forever.
GOTCHA_PROFILE_RETENTION_DAYS=7

# Structured logs retention. Logs are voluminous, so the default is shorter
# than spans. 0 keeps data forever.
GOTCHA_LOG_RETENTION_DAYS=14

# Retention for RESOLVED uptime incidents in PostgreSQL. Its own setting, not
# shared with events: an incident has no telemetry of its own, and the public
# status page shows 90 days of history. 0 keeps data forever.
GOTCHA_INCIDENT_RETENTION_DAYS=90

# Retention for deployment markers in PostgreSQL. Its own setting, not shared
# with events: deployment history is a separate axis with no telemetry of its
# own, and the table is written by the public ingest key outside any quota, so
# a bound is mandatory. 0 keeps data forever.
GOTCHA_DEPLOY_RETENTION_DAYS=90

# How often to look for ClickHouse telemetry of projects that no longer exist
# and queue it for deletion. 0 turns the check off (set it on installations
# where something other than gotcha writes into the same ClickHouse).
GOTCHA_PURGE_RECONCILE_HOURS=24

# Notification outbox retention (delivered/failed alert deliveries carry
# channel secrets in their payload — kept short by default).
# Must be >= 1: the outbox is a working queue, not an archive.
GOTCHA_OUTBOX_RETENTION_DAYS=7

# How many notifications are delivered at once. Serial delivery meant one
# dead webhook with a 30-second timeout delayed everyone else.
GOTCHA_NOTIFY_CONCURRENCY=4

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Quotas & edition
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Build edition: oss | saas. Controls the *default* for the quota
# variables below (oss -> 0/unlimited, saas -> 1000000/month). An explicit
# GOTCHA_DEFAULT_*_QUOTA below always overrides this default.
GOTCHA_EDITION=oss

# Default monthly ingest quotas assigned to new organizations.
# 0 = unlimited. oss default is 0 (unlimited) for all five.
# If you expose a project DSN publicly, set these to a real cap — the oss
# default of "unlimited" means a leaked/public DSN can ingest without bound.
GOTCHA_DEFAULT_EVENT_QUOTA=0
GOTCHA_DEFAULT_TRANSACTION_QUOTA=0
GOTCHA_DEFAULT_METRIC_QUOTA=0
GOTCHA_DEFAULT_PROFILE_QUOTA=0
GOTCHA_DEFAULT_LOG_QUOTA=0

# Maximum accepted size (bytes) of a single ingested event payload.
GOTCHA_MAX_EVENT_BYTES=1048576

# Per-DSN ingest rate limit: requests per second per project
# (token bucket, burst = 2x the limit). 0 disables the limit.
GOTCHA_INGEST_RATE_LIMIT=500

# How often (seconds) the metric threshold-alert evaluator runs.
GOTCHA_METRIC_EVAL_INTERVAL=60

# How often (seconds) the profile regression evaluator runs.
GOTCHA_PROFILE_EVAL_INTERVAL=300

# How often (seconds) the host threshold evaluator (disk/memory/load/silent) runs.
GOTCHA_HOST_EVAL_INTERVAL=60

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Privacy
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Byte ceiling for each ClickHouse writer buffer. Buffers grow while ClickHouse
# is unavailable, so a short outage does not lose telemetry — this bounds what
# that costs. Five writers at the default (256 MiB) add up to 1.25 GiB; on a
# 2 GB server lower it, as docker-compose.small.yml does.
#GOTCHA_MAX_BUFFER_BYTES=268435456

# Byte ceiling for the ingest queue, on top of its capacity of 1000 tasks. One
# event carries up to four raw JSON blocks of 256 KiB, so counting tasks alone
# left room for roughly a gigabyte of resident memory.
#GOTCHA_MAX_QUEUE_BYTES=67108864

# Run the periodic evaluators (performance regressions, metric alert rules,
# profile regressions) in this process. They ship with --mode=uptime and
# --mode=all by default, although they have nothing to do with uptime — which
# means that in a documented web+ingest split a metric alert rule looks enabled
# and never fires. Set this to true on exactly one replica of such a deployment.
#GOTCHA_RUN_EVALUATORS=true

# Per-project cap on DISTINCT values of open-ended fields (transaction name,
# environment, metric name, service, span operation). These values sit in
# ClickHouse sort keys and in the GROUP BY of pre-aggregates, so an identifier
# that slips into a name (/users/8812/profile instead of /users/:id/profile)
# turns a dozen endpoints into hundreds of thousands of aggregate rows — and the
# storage is shared by every project on the instance. Values beyond the cap are
# grouped under <cardinality-limit> rather than dropped, and the affected page
# shows which field blew up plus examples. See /docs/cardinality.
# 0 disables the limit entirely.
#GOTCHA_CARDINALITY_LIMIT=10000
#GOTCHA_CARDINALITY_WINDOW_SECONDS=3600

# Per-project notification ceiling. Alert throttling keys on
# (issue_id, rule_id), and a brand-new issue has no throttle row — so a sender
# using a unique fingerprint per event produced one issue and one notification
# per event, from a DSN key that is public by design. Suppressed alerts are not
# lost: their count is reported in a digest once the window closes.
# 0 disables the ceiling entirely (deliberate choice for trusted senders).
#GOTCHA_ALERT_BUDGET_WINDOW_SECONDS=3600
#GOTCHA_ALERT_BUDGET_LIMIT=50

# Zero out the reporting user's IP / email server-side before storage.
# On by default (PII scrubbing). These two also match FIELD NAMES in tags, OTLP
# attributes, span data, headers and query strings — see /docs/privacy.
GOTCHA_SCRUB_IP=true
GOTCHA_SCRUB_EMAIL=true

# Comma-separated key names (case-insensitive) redacted from
# tags/contexts/stack traces/span data. This EXTENDS the built-in denylist —
# list only your own keys, not the standard ones. Built-in default denylist:
#   password,passwd,token,secret,authorization,auth,cookie,api_key,apikey,
#   access_token,refresh_token,session,credit_card,card_number,cvv
#GOTCHA_SCRUB_KEYS=internal_user_id

# Exact names exempt from redaction. Matching is deliberately fail-closed: a name
# is redacted when it CONTAINS a denylist word, so author (contains auth) and
# tokenizer (contains token) are redacted by default. Under-redacting leaks
# personal data; over-redacting costs a debugging field and is reversible here.
#
# This is also the only way to drop a built-in key. It is checked BEFORE the
# email/IP rules, so an allowed name wins over them — but the match is EXACT:
# GOTCHA_SCRUB_ALLOW_KEYS=email unmasks only a field named exactly "email".
# It does not affect customer_email, nor events.user_email (that column is
# nulled by GOTCHA_SCRUB_EMAIL, a separate rule).
#GOTCHA_SCRUB_ALLOW_KEYS=author,tokenizer

# Optionally mask email addresses found in free text (message / exception
# value / span description). Off by default — conservative, since naive
# masking can corrupt SQL/URLs in free text. Only emails are masked, not
# phone numbers or other PII.
GOTCHA_SCRUB_FREETEXT=false

# Logging. Level: debug|info|warn|error (default info) — debug raises detail
# during an incident without a rebuild. Format: text|json (default text) — json
# is easier to feed into Loki/ELK.
#GOTCHA_LOG_LEVEL=info
#GOTCHA_LOG_FORMAT=text

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Security
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Signs OAuth state/session cookies.
# !!! The default value below is PUBLIC (it ships in source) and allows
# OAuth-based account takeover. Generate a strong random value for any
# non-localhost deployment, e.g.: openssl rand -base64 32
# The app refuses to start in web, all, ingest, and uptime modes against a non-local
# GOTCHA_BASE_URL while this is left at the default (escape hatch for
# unusual dev setups: GOTCHA_ALLOW_INSECURE_SECRET=1).
#
# Commented out on purpose: while this stays unset, docker compose falls back
# to the same insecure dev value, and the startup gate above refuses to run
# against a non-local GOTCHA_BASE_URL — a clear refusal instead of a publicly
# known key on a real server. Uncomment WITH a generated value as part of any
# real deployment.
#GOTCHA_SECRET_KEY=insecure-dev-secret

# Self-registration mode: open | invite | closed. The very first user on a
# fresh instance always succeeds regardless of this setting and becomes
# instance-admin (bootstrap).
GOTCHA_REGISTRATION=invite

# Language of outgoing notifications (uptime, regressions, performance)
# delivered by email/Telegram/webhook: ru | en. Recipients outside the UI
# have no per-user locale, so the instance operator picks one. Does not
# affect the web UI — there every user chooses their own language.
GOTCHA_LOCALE=ru

# Allow uptime checks and outbound webhook alert deliveries to target
# private/loopback/link-local addresses. Keep false on any multi-tenant or
# internet-facing instance (SSRF protection).
GOTCHA_SSRF_ALLOW_PRIVATE=false

# Per-path overrides narrowing the flag above. Unset -> inherit
# GOTCHA_SSRF_ALLOW_PRIVATE. The risk differs per path: a webhook echoes up to
# 1 KB of the target's response into the UI (a full internal reader), OIDC
# carries a client_secret — while monitoring an internal service is routine.
# Telegram's base URL is operator-set only (GOTCHA_TELEGRAM_API_BASE), not
# tenant-set, so it's routed through the same SSRF-safe client purely for
# defense-in-depth consistency (it-sec P2-1) — set this only if your own
# telegram-bot-api/proxy lives on a private address.
#GOTCHA_SSRF_ALLOW_PRIVATE_UPTIME=true
#GOTCHA_SSRF_ALLOW_PRIVATE_WEBHOOK=false
#GOTCHA_SSRF_ALLOW_PRIVATE_OIDC=false
#GOTCHA_SSRF_ALLOW_PRIVATE_TELEGRAM=false

# Trusted reverse-proxy networks (comma-separated CIDRs or bare IPs), e.g.
# "10.0.0.0/8,192.168.0.0/16". Only when the direct peer is in this set does
# the app trust X-Forwarded-For to derive the real client IP for the per-IP
# login rate limiter. Leave empty for direct exposure — otherwise a spoofed
# XFF header would bypass the limiter. Set it when running behind nginx/
# traefik/Cloudflare so the limiter keys on real clients, not the proxy IP.
GOTCHA_TRUSTED_PROXIES=

# Apply database schema migrations automatically on startup. Set to false
# to run migrations as a separate init job (e.g. so multiple app replicas
# don't all race to migrate at once) — the app then refuses to start
# against a schema that isn't current.
GOTCHA_AUTO_MIGRATE=true

# Domains and hosts that count as your own perimeter. Mail on these domains and
# webhooks on these hosts receive the full event details; everyone else gets an
# anonymized link back to the instance. Matching is on label boundaries, so
# corp.example covers mail.corp.example but not evilcorp.example.
#
# The host from GOTCHA_BASE_URL is trusted with no configuration, as are
# internal addresses (localhost, private ranges, .local/.internal/.lan). Set
# this when your mail or webhooks live on a different domain of the same
# organization — the parent domain of the instance is deliberately NOT trusted
# on its own, since walking one level up from gotcha.github.io would trust all
# of github.io.
#GOTCHA_TRUSTED_RECIPIENTS=corp.example,ops.corp.example

# Send error text (title/culprit/body) to EVERY recipient, trusted or not,
# Telegram included. Default is false (privacy-by-default): error text may carry
# personal data, and forwarding it outside your perimeter can be a cross-border
# transfer (152-FZ art. 12). Set true only if you have a lawful basis for it.
GOTCHA_EXTERNAL_CHANNEL_DETAILS=false

# Escape hatch: allow starting in web, all, ingest, or uptime mode with the default
# GOTCHA_SECRET_KEY on a non-local GOTCHA_BASE_URL. Development only —
# never set this in a real deployment. Unset/false by default.
#GOTCHA_ALLOW_INSECURE_SECRET=false

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Uptime & probe
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# How many uptime checks run concurrently (uptime|all modes), and how many
# concurrent checks a remote probe (--mode=probe) runs.
GOTCHA_UPTIME_CONCURRENCY=50

# Name of the built-in local uptime-check region.
GOTCHA_LOCAL_REGION=local

# --mode=probe only: bearer token this probe authenticates to the central
# instance with. Required when GOTCHA_SERVER_URL is set / --mode=probe.
GOTCHA_PROBE_TOKEN=

# --mode=probe only: base URL of the central Gotcha instance this probe
# reports to. Must be an absolute http(s) URL. Required for --mode=probe.
GOTCHA_SERVER_URL=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Agent distribution
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Directory with install.sh and prebuilt gotcha-agent binaries, served by
# GET /install.sh and GET /agent/{file}. Matches the path baked into the
# Docker image (ENV in Dockerfile) — on a standard docker-compose deployment
# leave this as-is. In a plain `go run` dev setup (no Docker build) this
# directory doesn't exist on disk, so both routes answer 404 with a hint
# instead of serving anything. If you build and run the binary yourself
# (systemd, no Docker) without ever setting up this directory, both routes
# 404 the same way.
GOTCHA_AGENT_DIST_DIR=/opt/gotcha/agent-dist

# Per-IP rate limit (requests/minute) on GET /agent/{file} (agent binary +
# SHA256SUMS downloads). A single install/update costs 2 requests, so the
# default below allows ~60 hosts/minute from one IP — enough headroom for a
# mass rollout (Ansible/Terraform) or fleet-wide update behind one NAT/egress
# address. Raise it further if your fleet is larger and shares one IP.
# 0 (or negative) removes the limit entirely, same "0 = unbounded" convention
# as *_RETENTION_DAYS below.
GOTCHA_AGENT_DIST_RATE_PER_MIN=120

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# OAuth / SSO
# Each provider is enabled independently; enabling one without its
# required secrets refuses to start.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Generic OIDC provider.
GOTCHA_OIDC_ENABLED=false
GOTCHA_OIDC_ISSUER=
GOTCHA_OIDC_CLIENT_ID=
GOTCHA_OIDC_CLIENT_SECRET=
GOTCHA_OIDC_SCOPES=
GOTCHA_OIDC_NAME=

# Yandex ID.
GOTCHA_YANDEX_ENABLED=false
GOTCHA_YANDEX_CLIENT_ID=
GOTCHA_YANDEX_CLIENT_SECRET=

# VK ID.
GOTCHA_VK_ENABLED=false
GOTCHA_VK_CLIENT_ID=
GOTCHA_VK_CLIENT_SECRET=
